---
title: Cited-answer legal research, and realtime voice agents
org: TaxCognitio · Cliqo
period: 2025–2026
role: Founding engineer on the legal research assistant, through its first three milestones. The production platform and evaluation suite that replaced it were built by a colleague afterwards and are not my work. The voice gateway is mine.
---

Two systems, both private company code, described here in architecture only. I have grouped
them because they share a problem: a model needs access to more sources, or more tools, than you
can usefully put in front of it at once.

## A research assistant that has to cite

A retrieval system over Singapore tax and legal material — statutes, court judgments,
commentaries and tax-authority guidance — answering with citations, across several
jurisdictions and at three depths of search.

For legal research an uncited answer is worse than no answer, because it cannot be checked and
it reads as authoritative. That constraint shaped the retrieval rather than the output format.

**Sources are not interchangeable, so the retrieval was not flat.** A statute, a judgment and a
practitioner commentary differ in authority and in how they are cited, and collapsing them into
one index loses that. I built per-source retrieval instead, each source bound to its own
namespace with its own retrieval depth, reranking behaviour, similarity cutoff and metadata
filters, with a selection step choosing among them. A later revision organised them into an
explicit source hierarchy.

**Statute lookup turned out to be two problems.** "What does section 10(1)(b) say" is exact
retrieval — the user has named the thing, and similarity search is the wrong tool, since a
near-miss returns a neighbouring subsection with quiet confidence. "What provision covers this
situation" is genuinely a search problem. I split the statute engine accordingly: graph
traversal over a knowledge graph of the legislation for lookup by reference, vector search for
lookup by content. Case law went the other way — retrieval over whole judgments rather than
chunks, because a holding does not survive being cut into fragments.

The agent loop used a ReAct pattern with a custom instruction to retry a different source when a
tool came back empty, rather than concluding that nothing existed. Models were self-hosted at
this stage — open-weight models served locally, with an embedding and reranking service
alongside them — chosen for cost control and for keeping client material in-house, with a
backup model and retry path when a service was unavailable. Each engine was containerised
separately and ran on GPU hosts.

**Where my work ends.** I took this through its first three milestones as founding engineer. The
production platform that replaced it — a different retrieval stack, hosted models, a deep
research mode, and the evaluation suite with its measured retrieval benchmarks — was built by a
colleague after I moved off the project. Those numbers are not mine to present, and I do not
have measured results from my own period on it.

## A voice agent with too many tools

A speech-to-speech assistant over a set of integrations — calendar, mail, messaging and social
platforms — exposed through MCP servers.

The naive approach is to hand the model every tool from every server. That fails in two ways at
once: the tool list crowds the context, and a realtime model with dozens of similar tool names
picks the wrong one with some regularity.

So the model sees **three meta-tools** instead of the full inventory: search for a capability,
request the schema for a specific tool, then call it. Behind the search sits query rewriting and
a vector router over the tool catalogue, returning names and descriptions only, so the model
gets a shortlist rather than a directory. Tool output is summarised before re-entering the
conversation, because a raw API response spends context the voice loop needs for the dialogue.

Two details worth mentioning because they came from watching it fail. The model sometimes
skipped the search step and guessed a tool name outright, so the gateway detects that and
re-injects guidance rather than returning a bare error. And the auth boundary rejects API keys
passed in the query string when running in production — one of the few places here with a real
unit test behind it, because it is exactly the kind of convenience that survives into
production by accident.

The gateway was migrated between realtime model providers during development, which is the main
reason the provider-specific handling sits behind an internal interface. It handles server-side
voice activity detection and barge-in, so the user can interrupt mid-reply. An earlier spike
evaluated a different speech-to-speech model with a hand-rolled bidirectional event protocol;
that one stayed a prototype and used push-to-talk, with no interruption handling.

**No latency figures.** The gateway has jitter-buffer and turn-timing instrumentation, and I
used it while building, but no results were recorded anywhere I can cite. I would rather say
that than reconstruct plausible numbers after the fact.

## Stack

Python, LlamaIndex, Neo4j, Pinecone, Ollama, vLLM, FastAPI. TypeScript, Node, OpenAI Realtime,
MCP. Docker throughout.
